From af3f7dd18a6b7d3b38e899ed693973beb6cb917a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:20:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/20] relay_metrics: Count tunneled directory response bytes
 by request type

Under a BEGIN_DIR flood, an operator needs to know what the relay spends
its bandwidth on. Stock tor counts directory bytes only in total, and
only for its daily history; the traffic counters can't tell directory
responses from relayed traffic.

Keep each tunneled directory connection's request type, in the 4 bits
that replace the request_counted flag (0 until the request is counted),
and count the response bytes its stream reads, where tor already counts
directory bytes, as tor_relay_begindir_response_bytes_total{type}. This
counts work done, not bytes the client received; like stock tor's
directory byte history, it misses the bytes still queued when the
directory connection closes, which conn_close_if_marked() hands to the
stream unread. An onion service descriptor that isn't found is a short
404, so "hs_get" bytes are almost all descriptors served.

Split the request type "other" by method into "other_get" and
"other_post". A relay that isn't public answers a POST with a 503 before
reading its URL; count it as "other_post" before answering, so that
every request we answer is typed before its response is written, and
only a connection that closes before its request is complete is counted
when it closes.

Part of #41339.
